\caption(The series on its own scale (left) and on the log scale (right). On the original scale the seasonal swings grow with the level. On the log scale they grow only slightly.)
\alttext(Two line plots against year from 1949 to 1961. Left: passengers rise from about 110 to 620 thousand with a summer peak every year, and the yearly swings widen from about 40 to over 200. Right: log passengers rise almost linearly from about 4.6 to 6.4 with yearly swings of similar height, a little larger in the later years.)

Traffic grew almost fourfold, from a mean of 127 thousand a month in 1949
to 476 thousand in 1960, with a peak every July or August. The yearly
range grew from 44 to 232, faster than the mean. The variation is roughly
proportional to the level, so the series is analysed on the log scale. The
last column is the range of the logs, which is close to the range divided
by the mean. It still creeps up from about 0.35 to about 0.47: the
summer peak became relatively more pronounced. But the log removes most
of the growth, and the model below treats what is left as noise.

> The model

>> Seasonal ARIMA

Write \(B\) for the backshift operator, \(By_t=y_{t-1}\). A seasonal
ARIMA\((p,d,q)(P,D,Q)_s\) model says that, after \(d\) ordinary and \(D\)
seasonal differences, the series is a stationary ARMA process:

$$
\phi(B)\,\Phi(B^s)\,(1-B)^d(1-B^s)^D\,y_t=\theta(B)\,\Theta(B^s)\,\varepsilon_t,
$$
\alttext(phi of B times capital Phi of B to the s times 1 minus B to the d times 1 minus B to the s to the D, applied to y t, equals theta of B times capital Theta of B to the s applied to epsilon t.)

where \(\varepsilon_t\) is white noise with variance \(\sigma^2\), and
\(\phi,\Phi,\theta,\Theta\) are polynomials of degrees \(p,P,q,Q\) with
constant term 1. A trend that grows steadily is removed by \((1-B)\), and
a stable seasonal pattern by \((1-B^{12})\).

>> The airline model and its autocorrelations

Box and Jenkins proposed \((0,1,1)(0,1,1)_{12}\) on \(\log y_t\). Let
\(w_t=(1-B)(1-B^{12})\log y_t\). Multiplying out the MA side,

$$
w_t=\varepsilon_t+\theta\varepsilon_{t-1}+\Theta\varepsilon_{t-12}+\theta\Theta\varepsilon_{t-13}.
$$
\alttext(w t equals epsilon t plus theta epsilon t minus 1, plus capital Theta epsilon t minus 12, plus theta capital Theta epsilon t minus 13.)

Its autocovariances \(\gamma_k=\operatorname{Cov}(w_t,w_{t-k})\) are sums of
products of coefficients whose lags differ by \(k\). Only lags 1, 11, 12 and
13 have such pairs:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\gamma_0&=\sigma^2(1+\theta^2)(1+\Theta^2), &\gamma_1&=\sigma^2\theta(1+\Theta^2),\\
\gamma_{12}&=\sigma^2\Theta(1+\theta^2), &\gamma_{11}&=\gamma_{13}=\sigma^2\theta\Theta .
\end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(gamma 0 is sigma squared times 1 plus theta squared times 1 plus capital Theta squared; gamma 1 is sigma squared theta times 1 plus capital Theta squared; gamma 12 is sigma squared capital Theta times 1 plus theta squared; gamma 11 and gamma 13 are sigma squared theta capital Theta.)

Dividing by \(\gamma_0\):

$$
\rho_1=\frac{\theta}{1+\theta^2},\qquad \rho_{12}=\frac{\Theta}{1+\Theta^2},\qquad
\rho_{11}=\rho_{13}=\rho_1\rho_{12},
$$
\alttext(rho 1 is theta over 1 plus theta squared; rho 12 is capital Theta over 1 plus capital Theta squared; rho 11 and rho 13 equal rho 1 times rho 12.)

and every other autocorrelation is zero. This is the signature to look for
in the sample: a spike at lag 1, a spike at lag 12, small echoes at 11 and
13 with the same sign as their product, and nothing else.

>> Conditional least squares

Given \(\theta\) and \(\Theta\), the residuals follow from the model by
recursion, starting from zeros:

$$
\varepsilon_t=w_t-\theta\varepsilon_{t-1}-\Theta\varepsilon_{t-12}-\theta\Theta\varepsilon_{t-13}.
$$
\alttext(epsilon t equals w t minus theta epsilon t minus 1, minus capital Theta epsilon t minus 12, minus theta capital Theta epsilon t minus 13.)

The *conditional sum of squares* \(S(\theta,\Theta)=\sum_t\varepsilon_t^2\)
is minimised, and then \(\hat\sigma^2=S/n\) with
\(\ell=-\tfrac n2(\log2\pi+\log\hat\sigma^2+1)\). `stats::ARIMA` minimises
\(S\) by differential evolution inside \(|\theta|,|\Theta|\le1.5\). The
exact Gaussian likelihood (R's default) also accounts for the unknown
starting values, which changes the estimates slightly.

>> Forecasts and their errors

Expanding the full model, differences included, as
\(\log y_t=\sum_{j\ge0}\psi_j\varepsilon_{t-j}\) gives the
\(\psi\)-weights, which solve

$$
\psi_j=b_j+\sum_i a_i\,\psi_{j-i},\qquad \psi_0=1,
$$
\alttext(psi j equals b j plus the sum over i of a i times psi j minus i, with psi 0 equal to 1.)

where \(a_i\) are the coefficients of the full AR side, differences
included, and \(b_j\) of the MA side. The \(h\)-step forecast error is
\(\sum_{j<h}\psi_j\varepsilon_{t+h-j}\), so its standard error is
\(\sigma\sqrt{\sum_{j<h}\psi_j^2}\). For the airline model,
\(\psi_j=1+\theta\) for \(j=1,\dots,11\). Each step ahead therefore adds
\((1+\theta)^2\sigma^2\) to the variance: the standard error grows like
\(\sqrt h\), and faster after a full year. Forecasts are made on the log
scale and exponentiated. That gives the forecast median, and the
exponentiated interval \(\exp(f\pm1.96\,\text{se})\) keeps its 95%
coverage.

> Identification

The next block differences the log series twice and computes the sample
ACF and PACF of \(w_t\) with `stats::acf` and `stats::pacf`. It compares the
ACF with the airline model's theoretical ACF from `stats::arma_acf`, using
the coefficients fitted in the next section.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arima_acf.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto y = dm::datasets::air_passengers().get_column<double>("passengers").values();
  for (double& v : y) v = std::log(v);
  const auto w = dm::stats::difference(dm::stats::difference(y, 1), 12);
  const auto r = dm::stats::acf(w, 36);
  const auto p = dm::stats::pacf(w, 36);

  dm::stats::ARIMAOptions o;
  o.d = 1; o.q = 1; o.seasonal_d = 1; o.seasonal_q = 1; o.seasonal_period = 12;
  const dm::stats::ARIMA model(y, o);
  const auto rho = dm::stats::arma_acf({}, model.expanded_ma_coefficients(), 36);

  const double bound = 1.96 / std::sqrt(static_cast<double>(w.size()));
  std::print("w has {} values; approximate 95% bounds for a white-noise ACF are +/-{:.3f}.\n\n", w.size(), bound);
  std::print("| lag | sample ACF | airline-model ACF | sample PACF |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t k = 1; k <= 14; ++k)
    std::print("| {} | {:+.3f} | {:+.3f} | {:+.3f} |\n", k, r[k], rho[k], p[k - 1]);
  std::size_t outside = 0;
  for (std::size_t k = 1; k <= 36; ++k) outside += std::fabs(r[k]) > bound;
  std::print("\nSample ACF values outside the bounds at lags 1-36: {}.\n", outside);

  std::vector<double> lags, acf_v, pacf_v, model_v;
  for (std::size_t k = 1; k <= 36; ++k) {
    lags.push_back(k);
    acf_v.push_back(r[k]);
    pacf_v.push_back(p[k - 1]);
    model_v.push_back(rho[k]);
  }
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .bars(lags, acf_v, "", dm::RGB{96, 165, 250}, 0.5)
                 .points(lags, model_v, "airline model", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 4.0)
                 .abline_h(bound, dm::RGB{107, 114, 128}, 1.0).abline_h(-bound, dm::RGB{107, 114, 128}, 1.0)
                 .y_limits(-0.5, 0.5).title("ACF of w").x_label("lag").y_label("autocorrelation"));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .bars(lags, pacf_v, "", dm::RGB{96, 165, 250}, 0.5)
                 .abline_h(bound, dm::RGB{107, 114, 128}, 1.0).abline_h(-bound, dm::RGB{107, 114, 128}, 1.0)
                 .y_limits(-0.5, 0.5).title("PACF of w").x_label("lag").y_label("partial autocorrelation"));
  layout.size(1200, 440).save_svg("build/arima_acf.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
w has 131 values; approximate 95% bounds for a white-noise ACF are +/-0.171.

| lag | sample ACF | airline-model ACF | sample PACF |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | -0.341 | -0.330 | -0.341 |
| 2 | +0.105 | +0.000 | -0.013 |
| 3 | -0.202 | +0.000 | -0.193 |
| 4 | +0.021 | +0.000 | -0.125 |
| 5 | +0.056 | +0.000 | +0.033 |
| 6 | +0.031 | +0.000 | +0.035 |
| 7 | -0.056 | +0.000 | -0.060 |
| 8 | -0.001 | +0.000 | -0.020 |
| 9 | +0.176 | +0.000 | +0.226 |
| 10 | -0.076 | +0.000 | +0.043 |
| 11 | +0.064 | +0.142 | +0.047 |
| 12 | -0.387 | -0.431 | -0.339 |
| 13 | +0.152 | +0.142 | -0.109 |
| 14 | -0.058 | +0.000 | -0.077 |

Sample ACF values outside the bounds at lags 1-36: 6.
\image(figures/arima_acf.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: sample autocorrelations of \(w_t=(1-B)(1-B^{12})\log y_t\) (bars), the fitted airline model's theoretical values (red points), and approximate 95% bounds for white noise (grey). Right: sample partial autocorrelations.)
\alttext(Two bar charts over lags 1 to 36. Left, the sample ACF has large negative bars of about minus 0.34 at lag 1 and minus 0.39 at lag 12, and smaller bars crossing the grey bounds at plus and minus 0.17 at lags 3, 9, 23 and 32; the rest are small. Red model points are zero except at lags 1, 11, 12 and 13, where they sit near the bars. Right, the PACF has negative spikes of about minus 0.34 at lags 1 and 12, minus 0.19 at lag 3 and a positive spike of 0.23 at lag 9; the rest are mostly inside the bounds.)

The two biggest autocorrelations are at lags 1 and 12, both negative, as
the airline model predicts when \(\theta\) and \(\Theta\) are negative.
The fitted model reproduces them: \(-0.330\) against \(-0.341\) at lag 1, and
\(-0.431\) against \(-0.387\) at lag 12. The predicted echoes at lags 11 and 13
are \(+0.142\). The sample has \(+0.152\) at lag 13, a close match, but only
\(+0.064\) at lag 11. Six sample values out of 36 cross the white-noise
bounds, at lags 1, 3, 9, 12, 23 and 32, against about two expected by
chance. Lags 3 and 9 (\(-0.20\) and \(+0.18\)) are not part of the model. Box
and Jenkins judged these small enough to leave out. The residual check
below supports that choice. The PACF, which for a pure MA process decays
instead of cutting off, has large values at 1 and 12 and smaller ones
around them. It points the same way.

> Estimation and diagnostics

The block fits the airline model, prints the estimates next to R's, and
tests the residuals with Ljung-Box over 24 lags. Two parameters were
estimated, so the test has 22 degrees of freedom.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arima_residuals.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto y = dm::datasets::air_passengers().get_column<double>("passengers").values();
  for (double& v : y) v = std::log(v);
  dm::stats::ARIMAOptions o;
  o.d = 1; o.q = 1; o.seasonal_d = 1; o.seasonal_q = 1; o.seasonal_period = 12;
  const dm::stats::ARIMA model(y, o);

  std::print("| quantity | stats::ARIMA (CSS) | R arima, CSS | R arima, ML |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| theta (ma1) | {:.6f} | -0.377162 | -0.401828 |\n", model.ma_coefficients()[0]);
  std::print("| Theta (sma1) | {:.6f} | -0.572379 | -0.556945 |\n", model.seasonal_ma_coefficients()[0]);
  std::print("| sigma^2 | {:.6f} | 0.001389 | 0.001348 |\n", model.sigma2());
  std::print("| log-likelihood | {:.4f} | 245.0666 | 244.6996 |\n", model.log_likelihood());
  std::print("| residuals used | {} | | |\n", model.n_used());

  const auto& e = model.residuals();
  const auto lb = dm::stats::ljung_box_test(e, 24, 2);
  std::print("\nLjung-Box over 24 lags: Q = {:.2f} on {} df, p = {:.3f} (R: Q = 24.65, p = 0.3141).\n",
             lb.statistic, lb.parameter1, lb.p_value);

  const std::vector<double> used(e.begin() + 13, e.end());
  const auto r = dm::stats::acf(used, 36);
  const double bound = 1.96 / std::sqrt(static_cast<double>(used.size()));
  std::vector<double> lags, v;
  std::size_t outside = 0;
  for (std::size_t k = 1; k <= 36; ++k) {
    lags.push_back(k);
    v.push_back(r[k]);
    outside += std::fabs(r[k]) > bound;
  }
  std::print("Residual autocorrelations outside +/-{:.3f} at lags 1-36: {} (about 1.8 expected by chance).\n",
             bound, outside);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(lags, v, "", dm::RGB{96, 165, 250}, 0.5)
      .abline_h(bound, dm::RGB{107, 114, 128}, 1.0)
      .abline_h(-bound, dm::RGB{107, 114, 128}, 1.0)
      .y_limits(-0.4, 0.4)
      .title("ACF of the airline-model residuals")
      .x_label("lag")
      .y_label("autocorrelation")
      .size(860, 420)
      .save_svg("build/arima_residuals.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| quantity | stats::ARIMA (CSS) | R arima, CSS | R arima, ML |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| theta (ma1) | -0.377162 | -0.377162 | -0.401828 |
| Theta (sma1) | -0.572379 | -0.572379 | -0.556945 |
| sigma^2 | 0.001389 | 0.001389 | 0.001348 |
| log-likelihood | 245.0666 | 245.0666 | 244.6996 |
| residuals used | 131 | | |

Ljung-Box over 24 lags: Q = 24.65 on 22 df, p = 0.314 (R: Q = 24.65, p = 0.3141).
Residual autocorrelations outside +/-0.171 at lags 1-36: 1 (about 1.8 expected by chance).
\image(figures/arima_residuals.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Autocorrelations of the 131 residuals of the fitted airline model, with approximate 95% bounds for white noise.)
\alttext(Bar chart of residual autocorrelations at lags 1 to 36, all small, between about minus 0.2 and plus 0.2, with grey bounds at about plus and minus 0.17.)

The library's conditional least squares agrees with R's to every printed
digit, for both coefficients, the variance and the log-likelihood. The
exact maximum-likelihood fit moves \(\theta\) by 0.025 and \(\Theta\) by
0.015. Both coefficients are negative. In the airline model
\(\theta\approx-0.38\) means that this month's surprise is partly reversed
next month. \(\Theta\approx-0.57\) means that more than half of a surprise in
a given month is cancelled in the same month a year later: the seasonal
pattern adapts, but slowly.

The Ljung-Box statistic, 24.65 on 22 degrees of freedom (\(p=0.31\)), is
the same as R's. It uses all 144 residuals, including the 13 zeros where
the recursion starts, as R does. Only one of 36 residual autocorrelations
crosses the bounds, about what chance gives. The structure at lags 3 and 9
seen above is gone, so the model has captured the dependence.

> Forecasting a held-out year

A fit to all 144 months cannot be checked against the future. So the next
block refits the airline model to 1949–1958 only and forecasts the 24
months of 1959 and 1960. The forecasts are compared with two other
methods. Holt-Winters exponential smoothing (`stats::ExponentialSmoothing`
with additive trend) is fitted with additive and with multiplicative
seasonality. The *seasonal naive* method simply repeats the last observed
year.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arima_forecast.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace st = dm::stats;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto y = dm::datasets::air_passengers().get_column<double>("passengers").values();
  const std::vector<double> train(y.begin(), y.begin() + 120), test(y.begin() + 120, y.end());
  std::vector<double> log_train;
  for (double v : train) log_train.push_back(std::log(v));

  st::ARIMAOptions o;
  o.d = 1; o.q = 1; o.seasonal_d = 1; o.seasonal_q = 1; o.seasonal_period = 12;
  const st::ARIMA airline(log_train, o);
  const auto [f, se] = airline.forecast_with_intervals(24);
  std::vector<double> point, lo, hi;
  int covered = 0;
  for (std::size_t h = 0; h < 24; ++h) {
    point.push_back(std::exp(f[h]));
    lo.push_back(std::exp(f[h] - 1.96 * se[h]));
    hi.push_back(std::exp(f[h] + 1.96 * se[h]));
    covered += test[h] >= lo.back() && test[h] <= hi.back();
  }
  auto mape = [&](const std::vector<double>& fc) {
    double s = 0;
    for (std::size_t h = 0; h < 24; ++h) s += std::fabs(fc[h] - test[h]) / test[h];
    return 100 * s / 24;
  };
  std::print("Airline model on 1949-1958: theta = {:.3f}, Theta = {:.3f}. Forecast se from {:.4f} to {:.4f} (log scale).\n"
             "{} of 24 held-out months lie inside the 95% intervals.\n\n",
             airline.ma_coefficients()[0], airline.seasonal_ma_coefficients()[0], se[0], se[23], covered);

  std::print("| method | parameters | MAPE 1959-1960 |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| airline model, log scale | | {:.2f}% |\n", mape(point));
  std::vector<double> hw_add;
  for (auto seasonal : {st::SeasonalType::Additive, st::SeasonalType::Multiplicative}) {
    st::ExponentialSmoothingOptions eo;
    eo.trend = st::TrendType::Additive;
    eo.seasonal = seasonal;
    eo.seasonal_period = 12;
    const st::ExponentialSmoothing hw(train, eo);
    const auto fc = hw.forecast(24);
    if (seasonal == st::SeasonalType::Additive) hw_add = fc;
    std::print("| Holt-Winters, {} seasonality | alpha {:.3f}, beta {:.3f}, gamma {:.3f} | {:.2f}% |\n",
               seasonal == st::SeasonalType::Additive ? "additive" : "multiplicative", hw.alpha(), hw.beta(),
               hw.gamma(), mape(fc));
  }
  std::vector<double> naive;
  for (std::size_t h = 0; h < 24; ++h) naive.push_back(train[108 + h % 12]);
  std::print("| seasonal naive (repeat 1958) | | {:.2f}% |\n", mape(naive));

  std::vector<double> t_hist, y_hist, t_fc;
  for (std::size_t i = 84; i < 144; ++i) {
    t_hist.push_back(1949 + i / 12.0);
    y_hist.push_back(y[i]);
  }
  for (std::size_t h = 0; h < 24; ++h) t_fc.push_back(1959 + h / 12.0);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(t_hist, y_hist, "observed", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.5)
      .lines(t_fc, point, "airline forecast", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .lines(t_fc, lo, "95% interval", dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, 1.0)
      .lines(t_fc, hi, "", dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, 1.0)
      .lines(t_fc, hw_add, "Holt-Winters", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5)
      .abline_v(1959, dm::RGB{107, 114, 128}, 1.0)
      .y_limits(200, 900)
      .title("Forecasting 1959-1960 from 1949-1958")
      .x_label("year")
      .y_label("passengers (thousands)")
      .size(900, 500)
      .save_svg("build/arima_forecast.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Airline model on 1949-1958: theta = -0.318, Theta = -0.567. Forecast se from 0.0380 to 0.1607 (log scale).
24 of 24 held-out months lie inside the 95% intervals.

| method | parameters | MAPE 1959-1960 |
| :--- | :--- | ---: |
| airline model, log scale | | 8.38% |
| Holt-Winters, additive seasonality | alpha 0.236, beta 0.030, gamma 1.000 | 6.70% |
| Holt-Winters, multiplicative seasonality | alpha 0.309, beta 0.030, gamma 1.000 | 7.29% |
| seasonal naive (repeat 1958) | | 15.52% |
\image(figures/arima_forecast.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Observed passengers from 1956 (black), and forecasts for 1959–1960 made from data up to December 1958: the airline model (blue) with its 95% interval (light blue), and additive Holt-Winters (red).)
\alttext(Line plot of passengers from 1956 to 1961. The black observed series rises with a summer peak each year, reaching about 620 thousand in July 1960. After a grey vertical line at 1959, the blue airline forecast follows the seasonal shape but runs below the observed values, most clearly in 1960, while the light blue interval widens from about plus or minus 30 to plus or minus 150 and contains the observed series throughout. The red Holt-Winters forecast runs just above the blue one, slightly closer to the observed series.)

All three model-based methods beat the seasonal naive forecast by a wide
margin, because it ignores the growth. The airline model's forecast has
the right seasonal shape but runs low: traffic in 1959 and 1960 grew
faster than the 1949–1958 fit expected. All 24 actual values still fall
inside its 95% intervals, which widen from \(\pm7\%\) one month ahead to
roughly \(\pm30\%\) two years ahead (a standard error of 0.161 on the log
scale). Additive Holt-Winters is more accurate on this split, 6.7%
against 8.4%. Both Holt-Winters fits push the seasonal smoothing parameter
to its upper limit, \(\gamma=1\): each new month replaces its seasonal index
completely. This is how they follow the steepening summer peaks noted
above. One hold-out period is a single draw, so the ranking should not be
taken as general.

> Summary

- Logs turn the growing seasonal swings into constant ones, and
  \((1-B)(1-B^{12})\) removes the trend and the seasonal pattern. The
  differenced series has autocorrelation spikes at lags 1 and 12 and small
  echoes at 11 and 13. These are the theoretical signature of the airline
  model, \(\rho_{11}=\rho_{13}=\rho_1\rho_{12}\).
- Conditional least squares in `stats::ARIMA` reproduces R's CSS fit to six
  digits: \(\hat\theta=-0.377\), \(\hat\Theta=-0.572\),
  \(\hat\sigma^2=0.00139\). The residuals pass the Ljung-Box test.
- The \(\psi\)-weights explain how forecast uncertainty grows: on the log
  scale the standard error is 3.7% one month ahead and 8.6% a year ahead.
- Out of sample, the airline model's 95% intervals covered all 24 held-out
  months, but its point forecasts fell short of the faster growth of
  1959–1960. Holt-Winters was more accurate on this split.

```{cpp, eval="no"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

auto y = dm::datasets::air_passengers().get_column<double>("passengers").values();
for (double& v : y) v = std::log(v);
auto w   = dm::stats::difference(dm::stats::difference(y, 1), 12);
auto r   = dm::stats::acf(w, 36);             // and dm::stats::pacf(w, 36)
dm::stats::ARIMAOptions o;
o.d = 1; o.q = 1; o.seasonal_d = 1; o.seasonal_q = 1; o.seasonal_period = 12;
dm::stats::ARIMA fit(y, o);
auto lb  = dm::stats::ljung_box_test(fit.residuals(), 24, 2);
auto [forecast, se] = fit.forecast_with_intervals(12);   // log scale; exponentiate for passengers
```
